EEDI3m r10 (HomeOfVapourSynthEvolution/VapourSynth-EEDI3). VapourBox task #98,
docs/reports/task-98-per-thread-buffer-maps.

The filter is fmParallel and keeps scratch buffers per worker thread in
std::unordered_map<std::thread::id, ...> members of the filter instance. r8
held one mutex around the whole check-insert-lookup; r9.1's 61e14ab ("Use
shared_mutex for unordered_map") locks the insert (unique) and the lookup
(shared) but runs the first count() with no lock, so it can read the map while
another thread inserts into it or rehashes it. Two threads starting their
first frame of an instance together corrupt the map or lose an insert, and the
kernel's .at() then throws std::out_of_range inside a noexcept function:
std::terminate, so vspipe aborts ("unordered_map::at: key not found"; on
Windows the UCRT's abort is a fail-fast, exit 0xC0000409). Not reproduced (0
aborts in 10 graphs of 1000 instances on an M1): reserve(numThreads) makes a
rehash rare. The patch moves that count() under the shared lock; nothing else
changes. Windows and macOS x64 ship r8 and are not patched.

The patch locks the map: getFrame checks for the thread's buffers under a
shared lock and allocates them under a unique lock (only the calling thread
inserts its own key), and the kernels look them up through threadBuffer(),
under the shared lock. The buffers, their reuse and so the output are
unchanged, sample for sample. It also appends "(VapourBox: locked per-thread
buffers, task #98)" to the plugin's name, which is how a test and the deps
scripts recognise a patched build. No upstream release fixes this.

Made by docs/reports/task-98-per-thread-buffer-maps/scripts/make_patches.py
and applied by download-deps-{macos,linux}.sh and download-deps-windows.ps1
right after the clone; each build fails if it stops applying.

